if you're going to tell the story of a  metal drummer who's losing his hearing  how do you dramatize that and connect  the audience with the character's  experience  one of the beautiful things about film  is that as a medium that combines both  sound and picture it's actually possible  to modify the sound the audience is  hearing  so that it mimics the perspective of  someone with hearing loss  for director darius martyr and  supervising sound editor nicholas becker  these representations of ruben's hearing  loss were based on extensive research  into how hearing loss sounds that  started a year before any other crew  were brought onto the film  this video is sponsored by nordpass  creating the soundscape of deafness is  extremely nuanced  but there are a few simple techniques  that are used to great effect  in the film this is alex knickerbocker  and chances are you've probably heard  his work at some point watching tv or in  a theater i've learned a lot about film  sound from his channel and so i reached  out to see if he would help me  understand the artistry that went into  the sound of the sound of metal  the two biggest factors in how we  perceive sound are frequency  and amplitude in moments representing  the beginning of ruben's hearing loss  things don't just sound quieter but are  duller and muffled  as we start losing our hearing we're not  just losing all frequencies at the same  rate it's not like turning a volume knob  down we're actually losing each  individual frequency differently  most of what reuben can still hear are  lower end frequencies and so these  frequencies are what we get to hear as  the audience when we're in his  perspective  some of the work to create this effect  can be done in post-production  you can mimic that simply by recording  your voice into a normal microphone and  rolling off all the high frequencies  until  only those lower frequencies are left  for example i could use an equalizer on  my voice to cut out the high end and  make it sound more muffled like you're  hearing now  but creating this effect as accurately  and richly as possible starts even in  how you approach recording the sound  there's a type of microphone called a  contact mic and it picks up  sound not through the air as a normal  microphone would  but through vibrations in solid objects  so the microphone needs to be in direct  contact with whatever object it's going  to record sound through  so what you end up with is a very  different perception of sound  because you're not hearing it travel  through air as normal it's coming  through very condensed  solid matter for example in this scene a  contact mic placed directly on the slide  would have a very different perception  of the sound of reuben  drumming than a typical microphone would  [Music]  this difference in perception that can  be captured with a contact mic  was an important piece of illustrating  rubin's experience of hearing loss for  the audience  [Music]  for nicholas becker sometimes even the  existing recording equipment wasn't  enough  i was able to build a small microphone  that you can put in your mouth without  it being broken  we were able to really recall all the  inner sounds from riz  racing heartbeats blood pressure tendons  moves muscle and bone movements  but creating a compelling sound  experience for the audience and telling  the film's story wasn't just about being  able to create an accurate  representation of reuben's subjective  perspective  one of the most important elements is  how the film uses the movement between  sonic perspectives  and the contrast between loud and quiet  moments in the film  [Music]  this is ridiculous one of my favorite  sonic choices throughout the entire film  is the use of contrast between  perspectives between  loud versus quiet between delicate  versus intense  with a film titled the sound of metal  you expect that to be  abrasive to some degree you expect it to  be really over the top  the opening sequence of the concert is  just that it's extremely aggressive it's  really intense sound and then  the second you get away from that it  cuts to  a peaceful morning inside of basically a  camper out in the middle of a parking  lot  using that kind of contrast just in  perspective changes  really draws your attention to how loud  something is or how aggressive something  is and then gives you a point of  reference that's  totally separate from what that was just  like so  it guides the audience's attention very  effectively that contrast between the  loud concert  and the quiet environment the next day  makes us more aware of the sound in the  camper and reuben's environment  without drawing too much attention to  the sound design itself  you have all of these little subtle  details of the birds outside  the atmospheres feel just a little bit  more pristine and  even down to the foley or the production  recordings  of the blender going in the morning and  all the little details  of the foley as the characters move  around the trailer itself  it's all this stuff that you wouldn't  ordinarily think of  drawing our attention to these sounds is  important because it increases our  awareness of their absence the next day  as reuben begins to lose his hearing  the same idea can be applied when you're  switching back and forth between  perspectives from  the you know normal perception of of  the world around you it's probably a two  out of ten in terms of his hearing  he can't he's having trouble even  communicating with me versus what it's  like to perceive those same things  without being able to hear them  having that contrast makes the starkness  of silence  that much more intense and that much  more heavy the film spends a lot of time  inside reuben's auditory perspective  at the beginning often tying these  moments to more subjective shots that  places inside reuben's perspective  visually  but as the story moves along more time  is spent  back in the auditory perspective of full  hearing and the film only uses reuben's  auditory perspective  for emphasis but because the film  returns mostly to the perspective of  full hearing  during reuben's stay at the deaf  community  what there are lots of scenes with no  dialogue or music  and because of that the sounds from the  environment and the movements of the  characters take  more of a center stage than they would  in most films  the sound team did a really effective  job with this film in the quiet and  subtle moments because  from a technical perspective once you  start taking away music you take away  a lot of you know over-the-top action  stuff sonically there's not really a  whole lot to hide  behind  so you better make sure that your  production sound is really clean you  don't have any traffic you don't have  anything that isn't going on in picture  to distract  and you have to make sure that all of  the background layers of sounds from  the birds outside  to maybe the creaking of the old house  to the wind coming through you know  maybe a drafty point a window  all of those little details need to be  exactly right one of the hardest  balances to find in those  moments is how loud you play certain  foley movements you know even  the smallest most delicate cloth  movements if you don't hear those to a  degree  a film sounds lifeless and you don't  notice it until it's not  there so during these scenes when you  have a whole group of people  not talking they're just signing to each  other  the foley of cloth movement  the foley of the actual sound of your  hands  you know if you get really close into a  microphone  you can hear that you can hear that  there is some movement to it but  obviously if you  overplay that it's going to sound  unnatural and draw attention to itself  so that balance that they struck  in those scenes had to have been  extremely delicate  ultimately the sound design plays a big  role in the emotional story arc and  ruben's character arc in the film  the contrast we talked about earlier of  the loud distorted sounds of the metal  concert  against both the quieter sounds of the  next day and reuben's hearing loss  sets up a kind of sonic motif that  bookends the story  spoilers for the end of the movie ahead  once rubin gets cochlear implants the  film's interpretation of what this  sounds like  while less pleasant and musical than the  concert music the film opens with  [Music]  bears some striking resemblance  nonetheless  through distortion and compression  everything sounds overwhelming  metallic and grating at both the  beginning and the end of the film  there's a shift from a sound of metal to  quiet  and it's reuben's change in how he  perceives these two shifts  that marks his character's development  the first shift is something lost  and the second through the acceptance  reuben has learned  is something gained huge thank you once  again to alex for his help on this video  he makes some of the best educational  content on youtube about sound  and thank you to my sponsor nordpass for  sponsoring this video  i'm very excited to tell you about  nordpass because i genuinely believe  everyone should be using a password  manager on the internet we've all had to  deal with the headache  that is having to manage our passwords  writing your passwords down or storing  them in your browser is inconvenient  and insecure and using the same easy to  remember passwords over and over again  for multiple websites puts you at a huge  security risk but you can solve both of  these problems at the same time  increase your security and make your  life easier by using  nordpass i've been using a password  manager for years and i would hate to  have to go back to not using one  you can use nor pass for free on one  device but premium is super affordable  it allows you to access your password  securely  on up to six devices it lets you share  passwords like a netflix password with  other people securely and it  automatically checks y